.TH FERROR 3S
.SH NAME
feof, ferror, clearerr, fileno \- stream status inquiries
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B int feof(FILE *stream);
.PP
.B int ferror(FILE *stream);
.PP
.B void clearerr(FILE *stream);
.PP
.B int fileno(FILE *stream);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Feof
returns non-zero when end of file is read on the named input
.I stream,
otherwise zero.
.PP
.I Ferror
returns non-zero when an error has occurred reading or writing
the named
.I stream,
otherwise zero.
Unless cleared by
.I clearerr,
the error indication lasts until
the stream is closed.
.PP
.I Clearerr
resets the error indication on the named
.I stream.
.B Note:
v7's own page spelled it
.I clrerr
in the text and
.I clearerr
in the synopsis.  The name is
.IR clearerr ;
the source file is called
.I clrerr.c
because the object directory is flat.
.PP
.I Fileno
returns the integer file descriptor
associated with the
.I stream,
see
.IR  open (2).
It is POSIX rather than C11, and is here because v7 has it and v7 code uses it.
.PP
These functions
are implemented as macros;
they cannot be redeclared.
.B Note:
.IR feof ,
.I ferror
and
.I clearerr
also exist as real functions, which is C11 sec 7.1.4 \- a macro may be suppressed
by parenthesising the name, and there must be something to call when it is.  Each
of the three evaluates its argument exactly once, unlike
.IR getc (3)
and
.IR putc (3).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
fopen(3), open(2), stdio(3)
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/stdio/feof.c, ferror.c, clrerr.c
